[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m ### Description
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m As hex values can include letters `A` through to `F`, certain English words can be spelled out, such as `CAFE`, `BEEF`, or `FACADE`.
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m This vocabulary can be extended by using numbers to represent other letters, such as `5EAF00D`, or `DEC0DE5`.
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m Given a string, your task is to return the decimal sum of all words in the string that can be interpreted as such hex values.
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m ### Example
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m Working with the string `BAG OF BEES`:  
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m * `BAG` ==> `0` as it is not a valid hex value  
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m * `OF` ==> `0F` ==> `15`  
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m * `BEES` ==> `BEE5` ==> `48869`
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m So `hex_word_sum('BAG OF BEES')` returns the sum of these, `48884`.
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m ### Notes
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m * Inputs are all uppercase and contain no punctuation
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m * `0` can be substituted for `O`
[36m(main_task pid=13624)[0m * `5` can be substituted for `S`
